  "rendering_judgements": [
    {
      "topic": "South completion_percent is the JSON literal 100, rendered 100.00 in digest.md",
      "judgement": "accepted",
      "reason": "JSON has no fixed-decimal type; the approved criterion reads 'completion_percent (2 decimals)' and digest.md's own method section says the percentages 'are JSON numbers rounded to two decimals' while markdown renders them with toFixed(2). 100 == 100.00 as a value, and its decimal exponent (0) satisfies the 2-decimal constraint rather than violating it. Every non-integral percentage (86.67, 81.82) does carry exactly 2 decimals."
    }
  ],
  "findings": [],
  "notes": [
    "Receipt metadata, not content, is off: digest.md is 1488 bytes on disk but invocation_receipt.json records size_bytes=1486. The 2-byte gap is the U+2014 em dash in the title line (3 UTF-8 bytes, 1 JS string unit), so the receipt recorded a character count rather than a byte count. digest.json is pure ASCII (467 bytes) and agrees exactly. Content hashes match, so this does not affect any digest value; it is a defect in the receipt's measurement, recorded here and deliberately NOT corrected in the artifacts.",
    "All three registered artifacts are now byte-verified: digest.json 95777cc3..., digest.md 86a99639... both equal the pinned probe expectations despite the receipt's sha256:null, and the procedure source 7d1dd3ca... equals both the qualified executable_sha256 and the receipt's expected value. The upstream gaps (null hashes/timestamps) were measurement gaps caused by a locked shell gate, not fabrication; nothing in those null fields was invented.",
    "Independent implementation differs by construction: decimal.Decimal with ROUND_HALF_UP over completed/scheduled*100 instead of JavaScript Math.round over completed*10000/scheduled with binary doubles, plus a regex table scraper for digest.md. Agreement is therefore not a re-run of the procedure's own logic."
  ],
